On a positive note, the Java version is set to `21` for both `java.version` and `maven.compiler.release`. This perfectly aligns with the requirement to keep the Java version at `21`, so 
that's one less thing to worry about in that regard.

The project structure is clearly a multi-module setup, with modules named `task-common`, `task-domain`, `task-service`, and `task-web`. This modularity is standard and expected for a 
project of this nature.

Looking at direct dependencies, I see `commons-text` at version `1.9` and `json` at version `20230227`. These are immediate candidates for potential vulnerabilities, so I'll be paying 
close attention to them.

A really good sign is the presence of the `maven-enforcer-plugin`. The fact that it's configured with `bannedDependencies` rules for things like `log4j`, `commons-collections`, 
